WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.902 --> 00:00:08.741
One of the most common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.741 --> 00:00:14.820
questions we get asked here on Journey to the&nbsp;
Microcosmos comes from all of you who are thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:20.207
of starting your own microscopic journeys and&nbsp;
want a little nudge in the right direction.

00:00:20.742 --> 00:00:22.133
The question of course

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.188
is what microscope should I use?

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:28.872
And that’s a big question to answer, actually.

00:00:28.872 --> 00:00:31.380
Our own journey through the microcosmos has meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.236
traveling through the lenses&nbsp;
of a few different microscopes.

00:00:34.236 --> 00:00:34.920
And that's why—

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:36.480
if you’ve ever seen our earlier videos—

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.625
you know that they look quite&nbsp;a bit
different from what we see today.

00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:43.773
And that is part of the fun of microscopy.

00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:47.154
A rotifer under any lens is a thrilling encounter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.154 --> 00:00:52.459
it’s just that the nature of the encounter&nbsp;
shifts with the light you shine on it.

00:00:52.459 --> 00:00:56.769
There are so many different ways to see the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.769 --> 00:01:02.917
and any microscope can be the right one if it's&nbsp;
helping you get started with your exploration.

00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:08.498
At the same time, there are thing that will make
a microscope more and less easy to use,

00:01:08.498 --> 00:01:14.398
more and less versatile, and we wanted to make
it at least a little bit simpler for you to kick off your

00:01:14.398 --> 00:01:19.950
journey by creating, what we think is&nbsp;
the best starter microscope out there:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.156
the Journey to the Microcosmos Microscope.

00:01:23.156 --> 00:01:27.650
You can buy it on our store,&nbsp;
and many of you already have.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.320
So, if you’re asking us what microscope&nbsp;
you should use to start your journey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:36.763
we have created the microscope 
we think is the best one.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:40.199
But of course, getting a microscope 
is just step one.

00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:42.600
There’s a lot to learn along the way.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:47.070
For those of you who are looking for a little&nbsp;
guidance on how to use your microscope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:51.960
or for those of you who are just curious about&nbsp;
what the microscope looks like in action…

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.486
well, that’s what we’re all here for today.

00:01:54.486 --> 00:01:58.464
All of the footage we’re showing for the&nbsp;
rest of the video is footage that James,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.464 --> 00:02:03.660
our master of microscopes, has recorded&nbsp;
using our Microcosmos microscope.

00:02:04.149 --> 00:02:06.000
And if you don’t have our microscope, that’s okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.200
most of the tips we’re talking about&nbsp;
today should help you out as you start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.223
your microscopy journey, no matter what&nbsp;
microscope you choose to start with.

00:02:15.242 --> 00:02:18.120
We’re going to focus on brightfield microscopy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:23.292
which means that we'll be looking at samples&nbsp;
that are illuminated entirely by white light.

00:02:23.292 --> 00:02:26.400
This is the most straightforward&nbsp;
technique in microscopy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.089
so it’s a great place to start and learn from.

00:02:29.089 --> 00:02:33.198
But just because it’s simple&nbsp;
does not mean it isn’t powerful.

00:02:33.198 --> 00:02:39.480
The intensity of the light can render many of&nbsp;
our organisms transparent, giving us a view of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:46.742
the world and structures inside their bodies, like&nbsp;
the cells floating around in our tardigrade here.

00:02:46.742 --> 00:02:50.187
Now there are times when that light might&nbsp;
shine a little too bright and drown out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.187 --> 00:02:53.238
some other things we might want to see,&nbsp;
so in the future, we might cover some

00:02:53.238 --> 00:02:58.426
other filters and techniques that will
demonstrate other ways to use the microscope.

00:02:58.426 --> 00:03:01.013
Now once you have your microscope set up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:06.712
the next step is to decide what part&nbsp;
of the microcosmos you want to explore.

00:03:06.712 --> 00:03:10.224
And the good news is the&nbsp;
microcosmos is just about everywhere.

00:03:10.224 --> 00:03:14.040
You can start out by collecting some&nbsp;
water from a puddle or a nearby pond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.620
or maybe even the saliva in your mouth.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:24.294
Anything that has water is like a little portal&nbsp;
waiting to be opened by your microscope lens.

00:03:24.294 --> 00:03:27.787
To actually open that portal, you will&nbsp;
need to prepare a slide for your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.787 --> 00:03:32.064
microscope by transferring a small&nbsp;
amount from your sample to a slide.

00:03:32.064 --> 00:03:36.749
The first few times, you might be tempted to&nbsp;you 
know, add a little too much water to the slide,

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:40.969
especially because it seems maybe a little 
improbable that  you’ll find&nbsp;something in

00:03:40.969 --> 00:03:42.937
a little droplet of water.

00:03:42.937 --> 00:03:47.443
But microbes are tiny, and so you 
probably will find something.

00:03:47.443 --> 00:03:51.900
And too much water will cause the light&nbsp;
to refract, which will make it difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.900 --> 00:03:56.516
to make out all of the spectacular&nbsp;
details lying within their bodies.

00:03:56.516 --> 00:04:00.863
So you’ll want to try and keep&nbsp;
the sample as thin as possible.

00:04:00.863 --> 00:04:04.734
But also, if there is something in there you
can actually see with your eyes,

00:04:04.734 --> 00:04:06.600
you shouldn't make it too thin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.500
because you might crush those organisms&nbsp;
when you add a coverslip, 

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:16.416
which might be a somewhat traumatic
beginning for your first foray into microscopy.

00:04:16.416 --> 00:04:20.100
It’s a bit of a Goldilocks situation,&nbsp;
so here’s an example of a correctly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.909
and incorrectly prepared slide&nbsp;
to help you see the difference.

00:04:23.909 --> 00:04:26.280
On the right, you see the water is seeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.366
beyond the borders of the coverslip&nbsp;because 
there was just too much water there.

00:04:30.366 --> 00:04:34.044
The good news of course is that if you&nbsp;
mess up, you can always try again.

00:04:34.044 --> 00:04:39.904
Our world is a nearly infinite source&nbsp;
of microcosmoses to experiment with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.904 --> 00:04:44.901
and every mistake is just a different&nbsp;
path than you originally envisioned.

00:04:44.901 --> 00:04:49.080
Now once you have your slide on the microscope stage,&nbsp;
the next thing you’ll want to play around with is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:55.675
how much light is reaching the slide because that&nbsp;
will influence the quality of the image you see.

00:04:55.675 --> 00:04:58.500
With too much light, all&nbsp;
the little details like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:02.329
ridges along this Pinnularia might get washed out.

00:05:02.329 --> 00:05:05.492
Now one thing you can do 
is just dim the light source,

00:05:05.492 --> 00:05:10.440
but the tool that’s really going to help you adjust the&nbsp;
amount of light is the condenser diaphragm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:15.084
which James is helpfully demonstrating&nbsp;
here with the help of his cat.

00:05:15.084 --> 00:05:18.420
As he pushes it one direction,&nbsp;
more light enters the diaphragm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:22.514
allowing us to see more of&nbsp;
the cat on the other side.

00:05:22.514 --> 00:05:24.780
Now, instead of a cat, let’s see what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:28.314
when you adjust the condenser&nbsp;
diaphragm on the microscope.

00:05:28.314 --> 00:05:31.449
Starting out, the amoeba in&nbsp;
our view is a little dark.

00:05:31.449 --> 00:05:37.261
But when James opens the condenser too fast, the&nbsp;
little details inside of it become washed out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.261 --> 00:05:42.801
turning the organism into like a blobby circle&nbsp;
that has few distinguishing features.

00:05:42.801 --> 00:05:48.840
But as he closes the condenser again, you can&nbsp;
start to see the scaliness of the shell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:53.488
and the fainter bits of amoeba&nbsp;
that peek out from within.

00:05:53.488 --> 00:05:56.700
You might also find your view interrupted by dust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.700 --> 00:06:01.227
and impurities coming from the surface&nbsp;
of the condenser or the light source.

00:06:01.227 --> 00:06:04.200
If that happens, all you have
to do is adjust the height of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:07.934
the condenser by adjusting the knob on the side.

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:10.805
And if you feel like you’re getting&nbsp;
pretty comfortable with the microscope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.805 --> 00:06:16.525
there’s a technique you can work on to take your&nbsp;
journey to a different level: oil immersion.

00:06:19.971 --> 00:06:24.360
We have a whole video explaining oil immersion&nbsp;
and how it helps you see up close and personal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:31.295
with some of the most spectacular organisms, but&nbsp;
one thing to know is that you need to be careful.

00:06:31.295 --> 00:06:38.210
Not all objectives can work with oil immersion, 
and if you&nbsp;use the wrong one, you might actually ruin it.

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:42.166
And this technique relies on&nbsp;
high-magnification objectives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.166 --> 00:06:45.741
so you’ll need to be careful&nbsp;
not to move too quickly.

00:06:45.741 --> 00:06:50.299
If you’re not careful, you can cause&nbsp;
your objective to crash into the slide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.299 --> 00:06:55.265
which can break the slide but also
more importantly damage the objective.

00:06:55.265 --> 00:06:58.440
To start, you’ll want to prepare&nbsp;
a slide like normal, you’ll just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:03.479
need to really make sure the sample is&nbsp;
very thin before you add the coverslip.

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:08.618
Then, take a drop of the oil that came with&nbsp;
your microscope and put it on the coverslip.

00:07:08.618 --> 00:07:16.206
Using the 100x objective, turn the focus&nbsp;
knob very gently until you see a clear image.

00:07:16.206 --> 00:07:19.659
Now I think that one of the 
true joys of the microcosmos

00:07:19.659 --> 00:07:22.315
is&nbsp;getting to share what 
you find with other people.

00:07:22.315 --> 00:07:24.840
In fact, while watching our hydra here,

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.540
James got so excited about something that he saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:30.937
that we’re going to be making&nbsp;
a whole episode about it soon.

00:07:30.937 --> 00:07:35.520
And fortunately, unlike the masters
of microscopes of past centuries, 

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:42.698
we have many tools that make it possible to&nbsp;
record our journeys and share them with others.

00:07:42.698 --> 00:07:46.020
James does this by setting up&nbsp;
a camera on a tripod so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:49.718
the camera looks through the microscope eyepieces.

00:07:49.718 --> 00:07:53.598
And you can do the same with 
a camera or even a phone.

00:07:53.598 --> 00:07:57.390
Our microscope actually comes with a&nbsp;
phone mount to make this a bit easier,

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:03.000
and if you record some fun microscopic&nbsp;
footage, we would love to see it!

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.940
You can share your footage with&nbsp;
us on social media, on twitter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:10.980
instagram @journeytomicro or we also have a&nbsp;
submission form now on our store where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:16.769
can submit footage to possibly be used in&nbsp;
a future episode of Journey to the Microcosmos.

00:08:16.769 --> 00:08:23.762
We love being able to share the wonders of the&nbsp;
microscopic world with you every single week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.762 --> 00:08:26.760
and we hope that this video helps&nbsp;
you out at the beginning of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:31.470
own personal journey through the&nbsp;
unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:36.470
If you buy the microscope, it also comes with
a series of videos that will help you on your

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:42.748
sample collection journeys, setting up the microscope, 
and using it in a bunch of different ways.

00:08:42.748 --> 00:08:47.417
Next week, we’re going to be posting another video&nbsp;
about our Microscope, but this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.417 --> 00:08:52.913
I’ll be on camera, doing a full unboxing&nbsp;
video of our Microcosmos Microscope.

00:08:52.913 --> 00:08:55.707
You’ll see what the microscope&nbsp;
will look like when it arrives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.707 --> 00:08:58.025
and I’ll show you how to set the whole thing up.

00:09:01.839 --> 00:09:05.700
There's a bunch of names on the screen&nbsp;
right now, and that's the names of some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:11.160
our Patreon patrons, the people who allow us&nbsp;
to do the thing that we are doing right now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:17.400
sharing something truly remarkable about our&nbsp;
universe, not just the fact that we are surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:24.000
by this invisible world that for the vast majority&nbsp;
of human history, was entirely unknown to us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.518
but also advocating for this as an activity, as&nbsp;
a journey that more people can participate in.

00:09:30.518 --> 00:09:34.286
So thank you so much to&nbsp;
all of our Patreon patrons.

00:09:34.286 --> 00:09:37.202
If you want to see more from our&nbsp;
Master of Microscopes, James Weiss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.202 --> 00:09:39.783
you can check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

00:09:39.783 --> 00:09:44.405
And if you want to see more from us, there's&nbsp;
always a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

